-- std/Io.bang — host IO as ordinary effects (ADR-0104, host-io-design §1).
--
-- These are PLAIN `effect` decls: `import Io` names them, `with Io_Console as c { … }`
-- installs a handler, `c.print(msg)` performs an op. Nothing kernel-new — IO is a
-- paradigm-as-library (ADR-0084, the moat).
--
-- WHY A MODULE, NOT THE PRELUDE (least-authority, os-inspiration §1): a program must
-- `import Io` to name these effects, so its `main` row is the capability manifest
-- (ADR-0093 D5) — the opposite of ambient authority. A dependency cannot quietly acquire
-- IO: the acquisition would change its signature and every caller's row with it.
--
-- SEPARATE LABELS, NOT ONE `effect IO { … }` (row-attenuation): each label is a
-- separately-grantable capability. `--allow=Console` grants Console without Clock; the
-- driver (Main.lean) resolves those names to their allocated labels via the program's
-- effect table (labels are `4 + decl-index`, not fixed — ADR-0104).
--
-- PAYLOADS ARE SENDABLE (ADR-0101 G7): `Str` is μ-data, `Int`/`Unit` are ground, so every
-- op payload and result serializes into the record/replay trace (host-io-design §3). An op
-- carrying a thunk or a cap would be an escape channel out of the handler AND would have no
-- faithful trace serialization — the Sendable constraint keeps both closed.

-- Console — line-oriented terminal IO. `print` writes a line to stdout (returns unit);
-- `readLine` reads a line from stdin. Both Sendable (`Str`/`Unit`).
pub effect Console { print : Str -> Unit, readLine : Unit -> Str }

-- Clock — wall-clock reads. `now` returns milliseconds since the epoch. The canonical
-- "same program, different answer per run": its recorded trace is one `Int` per read, and
-- replay reproduces THAT run's timestamps (host-io-design §3, honest-limits).
pub effect Clock { now : Unit -> Int }

-- ── SIM handlers (the DEFAULT runtime; `--env=sim`, verified-core semantics) ────────────
--
-- A handler is SYNTAX (`with L as h { … }`), not a first-class value in v1, so a sim cannot
-- be exported as a plain `let`. Instead this module documents the canonical sim clause
-- bodies; a program (or `examples/hostio-echo`) installs them at its use site. Each clause is
-- `ret`-shape (ADR-0095 D4): the ret-shape wall forbids effects-before-resume in a v1 clause.
--
-- Console sim — `print` swallows the line (returns unit); `readLine` returns a FIXED line.
-- A scripted, stateful readLine (feeding successive lines) wants carried-param UPDATE
-- (ADR-0092 D5, open) so the fixed-source form is the honest v1 sim (host-io-design §1,
-- the ndet-dst "honest v1 within the wall" discipline):
--
--     with Io_Console as c {
--       print(s)    => (),        -- swallow: the sim produces no real output
--       readLine(u) => SNil       -- a fixed empty line (or a scripted constant)
--     }
--
-- Clock sim — `now` returns a FIXED timestamp (deterministic, replayable):
--
--     with Io_Clock as k { now(u) => 0 }
--
-- The REAL handlers live host-side in Main.lean's driver loop (`--env=real`), do the actual
-- IO in Lean's IO monad, and RECORD each (label,op,payload,result) row so the run can be
-- REPLAYED under these sim handlers on the pure oracle — the invariant-#1 gate (ADR-0104 §3).
